  "rewritten_text": "Having adopted this perspective, I had little further hesitation. Assuming that during my journey I would encounter an atmosphere essentially identical to that at the earth's surface, I believed that, using M. Grimm’s highly ingenious apparatus, I could easily condense enough of it for breathing. This would eliminate the primary obstacle to a trip to the moon. I had certainly spent significant money and effort adapting the device for this purpose, and I felt confident in its successful application, provided I could finish the voyage within a reasonable timeframe. This brings me back to the potential speed of travel.\n\nIt is true that balloons, during the initial stage of their ascent from the earth, are known to rise at a relatively moderate speed. Now, the lifting power depends entirely on the gas in the balloon being lighter than the surrounding air; at first glance, it does not seem likely that as the balloon gains altitude and enters atmospheric layers of rapidly decreasing density, the initial velocity would increase. On the other hand, I was unaware of any recorded ascent where a decrease in the absolute rate of climb was apparent, even though that should have happened—if for no other reason than the leakage of gas through poorly constructed balloons sealed with nothing better than standard varnish. It seemed, therefore, that the effect of such leakage was only enough to offset some accelerating force. I now reasoned that, provided I found the medium I had imagined during my journey, and provided it proved to be essentially what we call atmospheric air, it would make little difference at what extreme level of thinness I discovered it—at least regarding my ability to ascend—because the gas in the balloon would not only be subject to similar thinning (which I could manage by releasing enough gas to prevent an explosion), but, being what it was, it would in any case remain specifically lighter than any mixture of nitrogen and oxygen. Meanwhile, the force of gravity would constantly decrease in proportion to the square of the distance, and thus, with a tremendously accelerating velocity, I would eventually reach those distant regions where the earth’s gravitational pull would be superseded by that of the moon. Based on these ideas, I did not think it necessary to burden myself with more provisions than would suffice for forty days.\n\nThere was still, however, another difficulty that caused me some anxiety. It has been noted that in balloon ascents to any significant height, in addition to the difficulty of breathing, great discomfort is felt in the head and body, often accompanied by nosebleeds and other alarming symptoms, which become increasingly troublesome as altitude increases."
}

