 over the water. We decided to release enough gas to lower our guide-rope and its attached buoys into the sea. This was done at once, and we began a slow descent. Within twenty minutes, the first buoy touched the water, and after the second followed shortly after, our altitude stabilized. Eager to evaluate the rudder and screw, we immediately engaged both to shift our heading eastward toward Paris. The rudder allowed us to change direction instantly, positioning us nearly perpendicular to the wind; we then activated the screw’s spring and were pleased to see it drive us forward as intended."
}

