  "rewritten_text": "We now continue with the journal, as transcribed by Mr. Forsyth from the combined manuscripts of Mr. Monck Mason and Mr. Ainsworth. The main body of the journal is written in Mr. Mason’s handwriting, with a postscript added each day by Mr. Ainsworth, who is currently preparing a more detailed and undoubtedly thrilling account of the voyage for the public.\n\n“THE JOURNAL.\n\n“Saturday, April 6th.—Having finished all preparations that might cause trouble the night before, we began inflating the balloon at daybreak. However, a thick fog weighed down the silk folds and made the material difficult to handle, so we did not finish until nearly eleven o’clock. We then cut loose in high spirits and rose gently but steadily, carried by a light north breeze toward the British Channel. We found the lifting power greater than expected; as we rose higher, clearing the cliffs and entering the sun’s rays, our ascent became very rapid. I did not want to lose gas so early in the adventure, so I decided to keep climbing for the time being. We soon let out our guide-rope, but even after it cleared the ground, we continued to rise quickly. The balloon was unusually steady and looked beautiful. About ten minutes after takeoff, the barometer showed an altitude of 15,000 feet. The weather was remarkably fine, and the view of the landscape below—always romantic—was now especially magnificent. The deep gorges looked like lakes because they were filled with dense mist, while the peaks and crags to the southeast, piled in a chaotic jumble, resembled the giant cities of eastern legends. We were quickly approaching the mountains to the south, but our altitude was more than enough to clear them safely. In a few minutes, we soared over them easily. Mr. Ainsworth and the sailors were surprised at how low the mountains appeared from the car, as great height in a balloon tends to make the uneven ground below look almost flat. At 11:30, still heading nearly south, we caught our first glimpse of the Bristol Channel. Fifteen minutes later, the line of breakers on the coast appeared directly beneath us, and we were officially out at sea. We decided to release enough gas to lower our guide-rope, with its attached buoys, into the water. We did this immediately and began a gradual descent. In about twenty minutes, our first buoy touched the water, and after the second one dipped shortly after, our altitude remained steady. We were all eager to test the effectiveness of the rudder and screw, so we put them to use right away to change our direction toward the east, aiming for Paris. Using the rudder, we instantly made the necessary turn, bringing our course nearly perpendicular to the wind. We then engaged the screw’s spring and were delighted to find that it propelled us forward exactly as we had hoped."
}

