 room. I then slept, and upon waking, I must have retraced my path, leading me to believe the circuit was nearly twice its actual length. My mental confusion kept me from noticing that I had started my journey with the wall on my left and finished with it on my right. I had also been misled regarding the shape of the space. While feeling my way, I had encountered numerous angles, leading me to conclude it was highly irregular; such is the power of total darkness on someone waking from a stupor! The angles were merely a few shallow indentations or niches at irregular intervals. The prison was essentially square. What I had perceived as stone masonry now appeared to be iron or some other metal, formed into massive plates whose seams created the depressions. The entire surface of this metallic enclosure was crudely painted with all the grotesque and unsettling imagery that monastic superstition regarding the grave has produced."
