  "rewritten_text": "I had been saved for the latter. My nerves had been so frayed by long suffering that I trembled at the sound of my own voice, making me a perfect candidate for the torture that awaited me. Shaking in every limb, I felt my way back to the wall, deciding to die there rather than risk the terrors of the pits, which my imagination now pictured scattered all around the dungeon. In a different state of mind, I might have had the courage to end my misery by jumping into one of these abysses, but now I was a complete coward. I could not forget what I had read about these pits—that a quick death was no part of their horrific design. Agitation kept me awake for many hours, but eventually I fell asleep again. Upon waking, I found a loaf of bread and a pitcher of water by my side, just as before. A burning thirst consumed me, and I emptied the pitcher in one gulp. It must have been drugged, for I became irresistibly drowsy almost immediately. A deep sleep fell upon me—a sleep like death. I do not know how long it lasted, but when I opened my eyes again, I could see my surroundings. A wild, sulfurous light, the source of which I could not initially identify, allowed me to see the size and appearance of the prison. I had been greatly mistaken about its size. The entire circuit of the walls was no more than twenty-five yards. For a few minutes, this fact caused me a great deal of useless worry; useless indeed, for what could matter less, given my terrible circumstances, than the dimensions of my dungeon? Yet my mind took a strange interest in these trifles, and I busied myself trying to explain the error I had made in my measurement. The truth finally dawned on me. In my first attempt to explore, I had counted fifty-two paces before I fell; I must have been within a pace or two of the scrap of fabric, meaning I had nearly completed the circuit of the vault. I then slept, and upon waking, I must have retraced my steps, which made me think the circuit was nearly double its actual size. My confusion prevented me from noticing that I had started my tour with the wall on my left and ended it with the wall on my right. I had also been deceived regarding the shape of the enclosure. While feeling my way, I had found many angles and concluded that the room was very irregular; such is the power of total darkness on someone waking from a deep sleep! The angles were simply a few slight indentations or niches at odd intervals. The general shape of the prison was square. What I had taken for stone masonry now appeared to be iron or some other metal in huge plates, whose joints created the indentations. The entire surface of this metal enclosure was crudely painted with all the hideous and repulsive images that the death-obsessed superstitions of the monks had inspired."
}

