model_content:
{
  "passage_id": "POE_WORKS_VOLUME_2_023",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "During our conversation, my companion recited several excerpts from an author on the subject of landscape gardening who is considered to have handled the topic effectively:\n\n“There are essentially only two modes of landscape gardening: the natural and the artificial. The former attempts to evoke the primitive charm of the countryside by aligning its methods with the local terrain, nurturing trees that complement the nearby slopes or flatlands, and identifying and applying those subtle connections of scale, ratio, and hue that, while obscured from the casual onlooker, are apparent everywhere to the seasoned naturalist. The outcome of the natural approach is manifested more in the lack of flaws and inconsistencies—in the dominance of a wholesome balance and arrangement—than in the production of any particular marvels or feats. The artificial mode possesses as many variations as there are individual preferences to satisfy. It maintains a general connection to the diverse architectural traditions. There are the grand paths and secluded spots of Versailles, the terraces of Italy, and a diverse, blended old English style that relates to domestic Gothic or Elizabethan architecture. Regardless of the criticisms leveled against the excesses of artificial landscape gardening, an infusion of deliberate art into a garden setting enhances its beauty significantly. This is satisfying to the sight, due to the display of structure and intent, and it also carries a moral weight. A terrace featuring an aged, mossy railing immediately conjures images of the graceful figures who walked there in past eras. Even the most minor display of art serves as proof of human attention and concern.”\n\n“Based on my previous remarks,” Ellison stated, “you will grasp that I dismiss the notion, stated here, of restoring the landscape’s original beauty. The inherent beauty is never as profound as that which can be added. Naturally, everything hinges on choosing a location with potential. The comments regarding identifying and utilizing subtle relations of size, proportion, and color represent one of those vague linguistic habits that merely mask imprecise thinking. The quoted statement could signify anything or nothing, and it provides no real direction. The claim that the true achievement of the natural style is found more in the omission of faults and contradictions than in the generation of unique wonders is a concept better suited to the limited understanding of the masses than to the passionate visions of a creative genius. The negative value proposed belongs to that timid school of criticism which, in literature, would deify Addison. In reality, while the virtue defined by the simple avoidance of wrongdoing speaks directly to the intellect and can therefore be defined by regulations, the higher virtue, which burns within the act of creation, can only be understood through its outcomes. Rules apply only to the merits of restraint—to the virtues of omission. Beyond these, critical theory can only offer suggestions. We might be taught how to construct a ‘Cato,’ but we are told in vain how to conceive a Parthenon or an ‘Inferno.’ Once the work is finished, however—the miracle achieved—the ability to appreciate it becomes widespread. The sophists of the negative school, who, lacking the power to create, have mocked the act of creation, are now the most vocal in their praise.”"
}

