{
  "passage_id": "POE_WORKS_VOLUME_2_027",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "After a convincing display of hesitation, and only once my persistent refusals had provoked him into sharp remarks that made my eventual agreement seem like a response to his irritation, I finally gave in. The outcome merely confirmed how completely my victim was ensnared; within an hour, he had increased his losses fourfold. For a while, his face had been fading from the ruddy flush caused by the wine, but now, to my surprise, it turned a truly ghastly shade of pale. I say surprise because Glendinning had been described to my careful inquiries as immensely rich, and the amounts he had lost so far, while substantial, should not have troubled him deeply, let alone affected him so severely. I assumed he was simply overcome by the alcohol he had consumed, and, more to protect my own reputation among my peers than from any altruistic concern, I was about to demand that we stop the game. However, comments from the crowd beside me and a cry of absolute despair from Glendinning made me realize I had brought about his complete ruin under conditions that, while making him a target for everyone’s pity, should have shielded him from the malice of even a demon.\n\nIt is hard to say how I might have acted next. The pathetic state of my victim had cast a mood of awkward somberness over everyone, and for a few moments, a deep silence reigned, during which I felt my face burn under the many intense looks of contempt or blame directed at me by the less depraved members of the group. I will even admit that a crushing sense of anxiety was briefly removed from my chest by the sudden and startling interruption that followed. The large, heavy double doors of the room were suddenly flung wide open with such forceful speed that every candle in the room was extinguished as if by a spell. As their flames died, we could just make out that a stranger had walked in, roughly my height and wrapped tightly in a cloak. The darkness was absolute, however, and we could only sense his presence among us. Before any of us could shake off the intense shock caused by this intrusion, we heard the newcomer speak.\n\n“Gentlemen,” he said, in a quiet, clear, and unforgettable whisper that sent a chill to the very core of my being, “Gentlemen, I offer no excuse for this conduct, for in acting this way, I am merely performing a duty. You are surely unaware of the true nature of the man who has tonight won a significant amount of money from Lord Glendinning at écarté. I will therefore provide you with a quick and certain method to gain this essential knowledge. Please take a moment to inspect the inner linings of his left sleeve cuff, as well as the various small packets that can be discovered in the rather large pockets of his embroidered morning gown.”"
}

