 out soon enough,” a man with a gravelly voice answered. “Perhaps you’ve arrived at a place not much to your liking, but I assure you, you won’t be asked for your opinion on your accommodations.” I was deeply shocked by such a harsh reply from a stranger, and I felt unsettled by the scowling, hostile expressions of his peers. “Why do you speak so sharply?” I asked. “Surely it is not the habit of the English to treat travelers with such lack of hospitality.” “I cannot say,” the man replied, “what the English custom may be, but it is the Irish custom to despise scoundrels.” As this odd exchange went on, I noticed the crowd growing quickly. Their faces showed a blend of inquisitiveness and hostility, which bothered and somewhat frightened me. I asked for directions to the inn, but nobody answered. I then began to walk, and a low murmur rose from the throng as they trailed and encircled me, until a sinister-looking man stepped forward, touched my shoulder, and said, “Come, sir, you must accompany me to Mr."
}

