  "rewritten_text": "I had previously toured the estates and country homes, frequently discovering residents driven by genuine kindness and prepared to offer significant support for the well-being of their tenants. Yet, this proved insufficient. The deep connection born from shared aspirations, anxieties, experiences, and goals was absent. The impoverished realized that the wealthy held distinct methods of survival unavailable to them, such as isolation and, to the extent possible, a life free from worry. They could not trust the wealthy, instead turning with increased reliance to the assistance and counsel of their own peers. Consequently, I determined to travel between villages, identifying the local leaders and, by organizing their efforts and broadening their perspectives, enhancing their influence and utility among their fellow villagers. Numerous shifts occurred in these informal leadership roles: removals and resignations were common, while passionate youths, hungry for activity and indifferent to peril, replaced the elderly and cautious. Frequently, a voice that everyone heeded was abruptly stilled, a supportive hand grew cold, a compassionate gaze shut, and the villagers grew more terrified of the death that had claimed a prominent figure, turning to dust the heart that had cared for them and reducing to absolute nothingness the mind constantly engaged in plans for their benefit.\n\nAnyone working for humanity must frequently encounter ingratitude, nourished by corruption and stupidity, growing from the seeds they have planted. Death, which in our youth had moved across the world like a nocturnal thief, now emerged from its underground tomb, empowered and draped in dark banners, as a conqueror. Many perceived a supreme Providence seated above his deputy throne, directing his arrows and guiding his path, and they lowered their heads in submission or at least compliance. Others saw only a random event; they attempted to trade fear for indifference, diving into debauchery to escape the painful pangs of extreme dread. Thus, while the virtuous, wise, and careful were engaged in charitable work, the winter lull created different results among the young, the reckless, and the immoral. Throughout the colder months, there was a widespread migration to London seeking entertainment; social constraints weakened; many who were previously poor had become wealthy; many had lost the parents who served as their moral guardians and guides. It would have been futile to resist these urges with obstacles, which would only have pushed those driven by them toward more harmful excesses. The theaters remained open and crowded; dances and late-night celebrations were popular—in many of these, propriety was abandoned, and the vices previously associated with a sophisticated society were magnified. The scholar abandoned his texts, the artist his studio: life’s serious pursuits had vanished, but the diversions persisted; pleasure could be extended to the very edge of the grave. All artificial pretenses vanished—death ascended like the night, and, shielded by its dark shadows, the blush of modesty, the reserve of pride, and the decorum of prudery were often discarded as pointless masks. This was not the case everywhere. Among nobler souls, suffering and terror, the dread of permanent separation, and the profound astonishment caused by this unprecedented disaster, tightened the bonds of family and friendship."
}

