  "rewritten_text": "I had previously visited manor houses and estates, often finding the residents driven by pure kindness and ready to help their tenants. Yet, this was insufficient. The deep connection born from shared hopes, fears, and experiences was missing. The poor realized the wealthy had ways to protect themselves that they could not share, such as privacy and relative freedom from worry. They could not rely on the rich, so they turned with even greater dependence to the support and advice of their own peers. I decided to travel from village to village, finding the local leaders and, by organizing their efforts and broadening their perspectives, increasing their influence and usefulness to their neighbors. Many changes occurred in these informal leadership roles; people were frequently removed or stepped down, and eager, reckless young people often replaced the older, wiser leaders. Frequently, the voice everyone listened to was suddenly silenced, the helpful hand turned cold, and the sympathetic eye closed. The villagers then feared the death that had claimed a valued leader, destroying the heart that cared for them and ending the mind that had been dedicated to their well-being.\n\nAnyone who works for humanity often finds that ingratitude, fueled by vice and foolishness, grows from the seeds they have planted. Death, which in our youth had moved like a thief in the night, now rose from his grave with great power, like a conqueror with a dark banner. Many saw a supreme Providence directing his arrows and guiding his path, and they bowed their heads in resignation or obedience. Others saw only random chance; they tried to trade their terror for recklessness, plunging into wild behavior to escape their worst fears. Thus, while the wise and good were busy with acts of kindness, the winter months had different effects on the young and thoughtless. During the cold season, there was a general rush to London for entertainment. Public standards weakened; many who were once poor were now rich, and many had lost the parents who had guided and restrained them. It would have been useless to try to stop these impulses, as that would only have driven people toward more harmful indulgences. The theaters were open and crowded, and dances and midnight festivals were popular. In many of these, proper behavior was ignored, and the problems usually associated with advanced civilization doubled. Students left their books and artists left their studios; the serious work of life was gone, but the amusements remained, allowing people to pursue pleasure right up to the edge of the grave. All artificial pretenses vanished. Death rose like the night, and under its dark shadows, modesty, pride, and social decorum were often cast aside as useless veils. This was not true for everyone, however. Among better people, the anguish, the fear of permanent separation, and the awe caused by this unprecedented disaster only strengthened the bonds of family and friendship."
}

