  "rewritten_text": "The busy eastern part of the city was silent. You could only see where warehouses had been ransacked, driven by greed or curiosity; bales of expensive goods from India, valuable shawls, jewels, and spices lay scattered across the floors. In some spots, the owners had guarded their property until the end, dying before their locked gates. The heavy church doors creaked on their hinges, and a few bodies lay on the pavement. A wretched woman, a victim of common brutality, had wandered into the dressing room of a wealthy lady; she had put on fine clothes and died in front of the mirror, which reflected her changed appearance to no one but herself. Women whose delicate feet had rarely touched the ground in their luxury had fled their homes in terror, only to lose their way in the city's squalid streets and die on the doorsteps of the poor. My heart sickened at the variety of misery I saw, and as I witnessed these grim changes, my soul ached with fear for my beloved Idris and our children. Would they, if they outlived Adrian and me, be left without protection in the world? So far, only the mind had suffered—could I delay the time when the delicate body and sensitive nerves of my wife, raised in rank and wealth, would be attacked by famine, hardship, and disease? It would be better to die at once—better to plunge a dagger into her heart while she was still untouched by misery, and then into my own! But no; in times of suffering, we must fight against our fate and try not to be defeated by it. I would not give up; I would defend my loved ones against sorrow and pain until my final breath. If I were defeated in the end, it would not be without a fight. I stood in the breach, resisting the enemy—the invisible, untouchable foe that had besieged us for so long. He had not yet broken through, and it was my duty to ensure he did not secretly undermine us and burst into the sanctuary of love where I worshipped daily. Death’s hunger seemed sharper now that his victims were fewer; or perhaps it was just that when more people survived, the dead were not counted as closely. Now, every life was a gem, and every breathing human was worth far more than the finest sculpture. The daily, even hourly, drop in our numbers filled my heart with sickening misery. This summer destroyed our hopes; the ship of society had wrecked, and the broken raft carrying the few survivors across the sea of misery was torn apart by the storm. People existed only in twos and threes. Man, the individual who could sleep, wake, and perform basic functions, remained; but man, who is weak alone yet more powerful than the wind or ocean when gathered in numbers—man, the conqueror of the elements, the lord of nature, and the equal of gods—existed no longer.\n\nFarewell to the patriotic scene, to the love of liberty, and to the well-earned rewards of virtuous ambition! Farewell to the crowded senate, filled with the voices of wise leaders whose laws were sharper than a Damascus sword!"
}

model_content:
{
  "passage_id": "SHELLEY_THE_LAST_MAN_023",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "Her courage only faltered when she feared danger for her parent, or worried that death might stop her from fulfilling the duties to which she was completely devoted. When we left Windsor for London, as the first step toward our final departure, we visited Lucy and organized the plan for her and her mother’s relocation. Lucy was saddened by the necessity of leaving her native lanes and village, and by having to move an infirm parent from the comforts of home into the empty, desolate world. However, she had been so well trained by hardship, and possessed such a sweet temperament, that she did not complain about what was unavoidable. Later events, including my illness and that of Idris, pushed her from our thoughts. We only remembered her eventually, assuming she was among the few who had traveled from Windsor to join the emigrants and was already in Paris. Therefore, when we arrived at Rochester, we were surprised to receive a letter from this exemplary woman, delivered by a man who had just come from Slough. He explained that while traveling from his home through Datchet, he was surprised to see smoke coming from the inn’s chimney. Thinking he might find companions for his journey, he knocked and was let in. There was no one in the house but Lucy and her mother. The mother had lost the use of her limbs due to a severe case of rheumatism, and one by one, all the remaining local residents had left, abandoning them. Lucy begged the man to stay with her, promising that her mother would be better in a week or two and they could then set out, but she insisted they would surely perish if left so helpless and alone. The man replied that his wife and children were already among the emigrants, so he felt it was impossible for him to stay. As a final resort, Lucy gave him a letter for Idris, to be delivered wherever he might find us. He fulfilled this request, and Idris was moved to receive the following letter: “Honoured Lady, I am sure you will remember and pity me, and I dare hope you will assist me; what other hope do I have? Please pardon my writing, as I am so bewildered. A month ago, my dear mother lost the use of her limbs. She is already improving, and I am certain that in another month she would be able to travel in the way you kindly offered to arrange for us. But now everyone is gone—everyone. As they left, they each said that perhaps my mother would be better before we were completely deserted. Three days ago, I went to Samuel Woods, who had stayed until the end because of his newborn child. Since they were a large family, I thought I could persuade them to wait a little longer for us, but I found their house empty. I have not seen a soul since, until this good man arrived. What will become of us? My mother does not know our situation; she is so ill that I have kept it hidden from her.”"
}

