  "rewritten_text": "We had looked forward to this moment with eagerness, for we had finally finished the most difficult part of our bleak journey, and Switzerland was close. Yet, how could we celebrate an achievement that felt so incomplete? Were these miserable people, worn out and wretched as they moved in a sorrowful procession, the only survivors of the human race? Humanity had once spread across the entire earth like a flood. It had flowed clearly from its original mountain source at Ararat, growing from a tiny stream into a vast, constant river, generation after generation. It had changed and diversified as it swept toward the final ocean, whose dim shores we were now reaching. At first, humanity was merely a plaything of nature as it emerged from the void into the light; but thought brought power and knowledge. Clad in these, the human race gained dignity and authority. It was no longer just a gardener of the earth or a shepherd of its flocks; it carried an imposing and majestic presence, with a history, famous ancestors, galleries of portraits, monuments, and official records.\n\nAll of this was over now that the ocean of death had swallowed the slowing tide and dried up its source. We had first said goodbye to the way of life that had existed for thousands of years and seemed eternal—the systems of government, obedience, trade, and family life that had shaped our hearts and minds for as long as we could remember. Then, we said farewell to patriotism, the arts, reputation, lasting fame, and the very idea of a country. We saw the end of all hope for restoring our old way of life, leaving only the weak expectation of saving our individual lives from the wreckage of the past. To preserve these, we had left England—an England that was no more, for without its people, what name could that barren island claim? We held tightly to whatever rules and order could best protect us, hoping that if a small colony could survive, it might eventually restore the lost community of mankind.\n\nBut the game is over! We must all die, leaving no survivors or heirs to inherit the earth. We must all die! The human species must perish; our bodies of exquisite workmanship, the wondrous mechanism of our senses, the noble proportions of our godlike limbs, and our minds—the throned kings of all these—must perish. Will the earth still keep its place among the planets? Will it still travel with steady regularity around the sun? Will the seasons change, the trees grow leaves, and the flowers shed their fragrance in solitude? Will the mountains remain unmoved, the streams continue their downward course toward the vast abyss, the tides rise and fall, and the winds blow through nature? Will beasts graze, birds fly, and fish swim when man—the lord, possessor, observer, and recorder of all these things—has passed away as if he had never existed? Oh, what a mockery this is! Surely death is not truly the end, and humanity is not extinct, but has merely passed into other forms that we can no longer perceive."
}

