model_content:
{
  "passage_id": "TWAIN_TOM_SAWYER_013",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "He stepped onto the land and walked into the forest. He sat down to rest for a long time, struggling to stay conscious, and then cautiously began the final leg of his journey. The night was nearly over. It was fully light by the time he reached the island sandbar. He rested once more until the sun had risen high, painting the vast river with its brilliance, and then he waded into the water. Shortly after, he stood dripping at the edge of the camp and overheard Joe remark: “No, Tom is loyal, Huck, and he will return. He won’t abandon us. He knows that would be shameful for a pirate, and Tom is too proud for that. He is planning something. I wonder what it could be?” “Well, the items belong to us now, don’t they?” “Almost, but not quite, Huck. The note says they are ours if he isn’t back by breakfast.” “Which he is!” Tom declared with a flourish, walking impressively into the camp. A hearty meal of fish and bacon was soon prepared, and as the boys ate, Tom told—and embellished—his story. They felt like a proud and boastful group of heroes once the narrative concluded. Tom then retreated to a shaded spot to nap until midday, while the other pirates prepared to fish and explore. CHAPTER XVI After the midday meal, the entire group went to search for turtle eggs on the sandbar. They poked the sand with sticks, and upon finding a soft spot, they knelt and dug with their hands. Occasionally, they would pull fifty or sixty eggs from a single nest. These were perfectly round, white objects slightly smaller than a walnut. They enjoyed a grand feast of fried eggs that evening, and another on Friday morning. Following breakfast, they ran and leaped across the bar, chasing one another and discarding their clothes until they were bare, continuing their play far up the shallow water against the strong current, which frequently knocked them over and added to their amusement. Occasionally, they would gather in a circle and splash water at one another, moving closer with their faces turned away to dodge the spray, eventually grabbing and wrestling until the strongest boy dunked his friend, leaving them all in a heap of limbs before they surfaced, sputtering and laughing. When they were worn out, they would sprawl on the hot, dry sand, covering themselves with it, before eventually rushing back into the water to repeat the game. Eventually, they realized their bare skin looked like flesh-colored tights, so they drew a circle in the sand and held a circus, featuring three clowns since none would give up that prestigious role. Afterward, they played marbles until the game lost its appeal. Joe and Huck went for another swim, but Tom declined, having realized that while removing his pants, he had lost the rattlesnake rattles from his ankle; he marveled that he had avoided cramps for so long without that mysterious charm."
}

