{
  "passage_id": "TWAIN_TOM_SAWYER_013",
  "condition": "modernize",
  "rewritten_text": "He stepped onto the shore and walked into the woods. He sat down to rest for a long time, struggling to stay awake, and then cautiously began the final stretch toward home. The night was nearly over. It was broad daylight by the time he reached the island sandbar. He rested again until the sun was high, casting a golden glow over the river, and then he waded into the water. A short time later, he stood dripping at the edge of the camp and heard Joe say: \"No, Tom is loyal, Huck, and he’ll be back. He won’t abandon us. He knows that would be a disgrace to a pirate, and Tom is too proud for that. He’s planning something. I wonder what it is?\" \"Well, the things are ours anyway, aren't they?\" \"Pretty much, but not yet, Huck. The agreement says they belong to us if he isn't back by breakfast.\" \"Which he is!\" Tom exclaimed with dramatic flair, stepping grandly into the camp. A hearty breakfast of bacon and fish was soon served, and as the boys ate, Tom recounted—and embellished—his adventures. They felt like a group of vain, boastful heroes once the story was finished. Then, Tom hid in a shady spot to sleep until noon, while the other pirates prepared to fish and explore. After dinner, the whole gang went out to hunt for turtle eggs on the sandbar. They poked sticks into the sand, and whenever they found a soft spot, they knelt down and dug with their hands. Sometimes they would pull fifty or sixty eggs from a single hole. They were perfectly round, white objects, slightly smaller than a walnut. They had a wonderful feast of fried eggs that night, and another on Friday morning. After breakfast, they ran whooping and prancing onto the bar, chasing each other in circles and shedding their clothes until they were naked. They continued their game far up the shallow water of the bar, fighting against the strong current, which occasionally knocked them over and added to the fun. Every so often, they would gather in a group and splash water in each other’s faces, moving closer with their heads turned to avoid the spray, eventually grabbing and wrestling until the strongest one dunked his friend. They would all go under in a tangle of limbs, coming up blowing, sputtering, laughing, and gasping for air all at once. When they were exhausted, they would run out and sprawl on the hot, dry sand, covering themselves with it before eventually rushing back into the water to repeat the performance. Finally, they realized their bare skin looked like flesh-colored tights, so they drew a ring in the sand and held a circus, with three clowns, since none of them would give up that prestigious role to the others. Next, they brought out their marbles and played games until they grew bored. Joe and Huck went for another swim, but Tom refused to join them because he realized that when he kicked off his trousers, he had lost the string of rattlesnake rattles from his ankle. He wondered how he had managed to avoid cramps for so long without the protection of that mysterious charm."
}

