ch to be gained from all these. But the artistic temperament they create, or at least reveal, means even more to me. To become a spectator of one’s own life, as Harry says, is to escape the suffering of life. I know you are surprised to hear me talk like this. You haven't realized how I have developed. I was a schoolboy when you knew me. I am a man now. I have new passions, new thoughts, and new ideas. I am different, but you must not like me any less. I have changed, but you must always be my friend. Of course, I am very fond of Harry. But I know that you are a better person than he is. You are not stronger—you are too afraid of life—but you are better. And how happy we used to be together! Don’t leave me, Basil, and don’t quarrel with me. I am what I am. There is nothing more to be said.\n\nThe painter felt strangely moved. The young man was infinitely dear to him, and his personality had been the major turning point in his art."
