osity, he would abandon them with a detached indifference that is not at odds with a truly passionate nature, and which, according to some contemporary psychologists, is often a prerequisite for it.\n\nIt was once whispered that he intended to convert to Roman Catholicism, and the Roman liturgy certainly held a profound fascination for him. The daily sacrifice, more solemn than any from the ancient world, moved him as much by its magnificent denial of sensory evidence as by the elemental simplicity of its components and the enduring sorrow of the human drama it aimed to represent. He enjoyed kneeling upon the chilly marble floor to observe the priest, clad in a stiff, embroidered dalmatic, moving the tabernacle veil aside with slow, white hands, or elevating the ornate, lantern-like monstrance containing the pale wafer that one might wish to believe is truly the “panis cælestis,” the bread of angels, or, dressed in the vestments of Christ’s Passion, breaking the Host into the chalice while striking his breast in penance for his sins."
}

