; in fact, according to some modern psychologists, it is often a condition of it.\n\nIt was once rumored that he was about to join the Roman Catholic Church, and the Roman ritual certainly always held a great attraction for him. The daily sacrifice, more profound than all the sacrifices of the ancient world, moved him as much by its rejection of sensory evidence as by the simple, primitive nature of its elements and the eternal sadness of the human tragedy it sought to symbolize. He loved to kneel on the cold marble floor and watch the priest, in his stiff, flowered vestments, slowly moving aside the tabernacle veil with white hands, or raising the jeweled monstrance containing the pale wafer that one might almost believe is truly the “panis cælestis,” the bread of angels. He watched as the priest, dressed in the garments of Christ’s Passion, broke the Host into the chalice and struck his breast for his sins."
