  "rewritten_text": "“On the other hand,” he continued, “if he really refuses to use the Rising Sun Lubricator, we will have to take his chains away. It would be impossible to sleep with that much noise happening outside the bedrooms.”\n\nFor the rest of the week, however, they were undisturbed. The only thing that drew any attention was the constant reappearance of the bloodstain on the library floor. This was certainly very strange, as Mr. Otis locked the door every night and kept the windows tightly barred. The chameleon-like color of the stain also sparked a lot of discussion. Some mornings it was a dull, almost Indian red; then it would be vermilion, then a rich purple; and once, when they came down for family prayers according to the simple rites of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, they found it a bright emerald green. These kaleidoscopic changes naturally amused the group, and bets were freely made every evening. The only person who did not join in the joke was little Virginia, who, for some unexplained reason, was always quite distressed at the sight of the bloodstain and nearly cried the morning it was emerald green.\n\nThe ghost appeared for the second time on Sunday night. Shortly after they had gone to bed, they were suddenly alarmed by a fearful crash in the hall. Rushing downstairs, they found that a large suit of old armor had fallen from its stand onto the stone floor. Seated in a high-backed chair was the Canterville ghost, rubbing his knees with an expression of acute agony on his face. The twins, having brought their pea-shooters, immediately fired two pellets at him with the accuracy that can only be attained by long and careful practice on a writing-master, while the United States Minister covered him with his revolver and called upon him, in accordance with Californian etiquette, to put his hands up! The ghost jumped up with a wild shriek of rage and swept through them like a mist, extinguishing Washington Otis’s candle as he passed and leaving them all in total darkness. Upon reaching the top of the staircase, he recovered himself and decided to let out his celebrated peal of demonic laughter. He had found this extremely useful on more than one occasion. It was said to have turned Lord Raker’s wig gray in a single night and had certainly caused three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses to quit before their month was up. He accordingly laughed his most horrible laugh until the old vaulted roof rang again and again, but hardly had the fearful echo died away when a door opened and Mrs. Otis came out in a light blue dressing gown. “I am afraid you are not feeling well,” she said, “and I have brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If it is indigestion, you will find it a most excellent remedy.” The ghost glared at her in fury and immediately began preparing to turn himself into a large black dog, a feat for which he was justly famous and to which the family doctor always attributed the permanent idiocy of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Honorable Thomas Horton."
}

