airs. This final indignity infuriated him, prompting him to plan one last attempt to reclaim his status and authority; he decided to confront the rude young students the following evening as his famous ‘Reckless Rupert, or the Headless Earl.’\n\nHe had not donned this costume for over seven decades—not since he had terrified the lovely Lady Barbara Modish so thoroughly that she abruptly ended her betrothal to the current Lord Canterville’s grandfather and eloped to Gretna Green with the dashing Jack Castleton, insisting that nothing could persuade her to join a family that permitted such a ghastly spirit to roam the terrace at dusk. Poor Jack was later killed in a duel by Lord Canterville on Wandsworth Common, and Lady Barbara passed away from a broken heart at Tunbridge Wells before the year concluded, so, by all accounts, it had been a resounding triumph."
