en dragged and the entire estate thoroughly searched, but with no result. It was clear that, for that night at least, Virginia was lost to them. It was in a state of deep depression that Mr. Otis and the boys walked up to the house, with the groom following behind with the horses and the pony. In the hall, they found a group of frightened servants, and lying on a library sofa was poor Mrs. Otis, nearly out of her mind with terror and anxiety, having her forehead bathed with cologne by the old housekeeper. Mr. Otis immediately insisted she eat something and ordered supper for the whole group. It was a gloomy meal; hardly anyone spoke, and even the twins were awestruck and subdued, as they were very fond of their sister. When they finished, Mr. Otis, despite the young Duke’s pleas, ordered everyone to bed, saying nothing more could be done that night and that he would wire Scotland Yard in the morning to have detectives sent down immediately."
}

